    <div class="rcc-column-steel-content prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <h2>RCC Column Steel Detailing Rules & Engineering Standards (IS 456 & IS 13920)</h2>
      <p>
        Reinforced Cement Concrete (RCC) columns are critical vertical compression members transferring gravitational loads and seismic/wind bending moments safely from beams and slabs down into the foundation footings. A column failure can trigger progressive structural collapse; therefore, strict adherence to <strong>IS 456:2000</strong> and <strong>IS 13920:2016 ductile detailing</strong> is paramount.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Longitudinal Reinforcement Rules (IS 456 Cl. 26.5.3.1)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Minimum Reinforcement:</strong> The cross-sectional area of longitudinal steel shall not be less than <strong>0.8%</strong> of the gross cross-sectional area of the column (<code>0.008 × Ac</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Maximum Reinforcement:</strong> Not more than <strong>4.0%</strong> (where bars are lapped) or <strong>6.0%</strong> (where bars are not lapped).</li>
        <li><strong>Minimum Number of Bars:</strong> A minimum of <strong>4 vertical bars</strong> in rectangular/square columns and <strong>6 vertical bars</strong> in circular columns.</li>
        <li><strong>Minimum Bar Diameter:</strong> The minimum diameter of longitudinal bars shall not be less than <strong>12 mm</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear Cover:</strong> Standard clear nominal cover is <strong>40 mm</strong> (or 25 mm if column dimension is 200 mm or less and bar diameter is 12 mm or less).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Cutting Length of Vertical Main Bars</h3>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Cutting Length = Floor Height (H) + Lap Length (50d) + Joggle Offset Crank (~80mm) + Starter/Hook Embedment
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lap Length:</strong> Standard compression lap length is <code>45d to 50d</code>. As per IS 13920 Cl. 7.2.1, lap splices must be provided in the central half of the column height, outside the critical end confinement zones.</li>
        <li><strong>Starter Bend from Footing:</strong> Starter bars originating from footing mats must extend with a <code>90° hook of 300 mm (or 16d)</code> resting on the footing mesh.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Ductile Lateral Ties & Confinement Zones (IS 13920:2016 Cl. 7.4)</h3>
      <p>
        Under seismic shaking, extreme moments and shear forces occur at the top and bottom junctions of columns. Special confining reinforcement (links) must be provided over a distance <code>L₀</code> from each joint face:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Confinement Length (L₀) = Maximum of [Larger Column Dimension, H ÷ 6, 450 mm]
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spacing in L₀:</strong> Not more than <code>D/4</code> or <code>100 mm c/c</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Spacing in Central Zone:</strong> Not more than <code>150 mm to 200 mm c/c</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>135° Seismic Hooks:</strong> All ties must end in a <code>135° hook with an extension of at least 10d (min 75 mm)</code> embedded into the concrete core.</li>
        <li><strong>Inner Ties / Diamond Ties:</strong> Under IS 456 Cl. 26.5.3.2, every alternate bar must be restrained by lateral ties if distance between bars exceeds 75 mm. For 8-bar columns, an inner diamond tie or cross links must be provided.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4. Site Thumb-Rule Rates for RCC Columns (CPWD Norms)</h3>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-2 border">Column Type</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Steel Ratio (% Volume)</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Steel Consumption Rate</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Typical Structure</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Light Residential Column</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">1.5%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">120 kg / m³</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Single-story residential house, boundary posts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Standard G+1 / G+2 Column</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">2.5%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">195 kg / m³ (160–200)</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Residential G+1, G+2 buildings, standard villas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Heavy Commercial / Multi-Story</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">3.5%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">275 kg / m³ (250–300)</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Multi-story apartments, shopping malls, high shear</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>5. Shuttering / Formwork Area Formulas</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rectangular Column:</strong> <code>Shuttering Area = 2 × (Width + Depth) × Height</code></li>
        <li><strong>Circular Column:</strong> <code>Shuttering Area = π × Diameter × Height</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQS WITH SCHEMA JSON-LD (SLOT FAQ) -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the thumb rule for steel quantity in an RCC column?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          The standard structural engineering thumb rule (CPWD / IS 456) for RCC columns is <strong>1.5% to 3.5% steel reinforcement by concrete volume</strong>. This translates to roughly <strong>120 kg to 275 kg of steel per cubic meter (m³) of concrete</strong>. For standard residential columns (300×300 mm or 230×300 mm), <strong>195 kg/m³</strong> is the recommended benchmark.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the minimum number of vertical bars required in a column?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          As per IS 456:2000 Clause 26.5.3.1, a rectangular column must have a minimum of <strong>4 vertical bars</strong> (one at each corner), and a circular column must have at least <strong>6 vertical bars</strong>. The minimum bar diameter allowed is 12 mm.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          Why is 2-zone ductile tie spacing required in earthquake design?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Under IS 13920:2016, lateral earthquake forces induce plastic hinge zones at the column top and bottom ends. Closely spaced ties (100 mm c/c) over the confinement zone <code>L₀</code> prevent premature longitudinal bar buckling and confine the core concrete during seismic shaking.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the purpose of an inner diamond tie in an 8-bar column?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          In an 8-bar column, the 4 corner bars are held by the outer rectangular tie, but the 4 intermediate face bars would be unsupported. IS 456 requires that every alternate bar be laterally restrained; an inner diamond (rhombus) tie or cross ties tie these intermediate bars together to prevent buckling under compressive loads.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the minimum clear cover required for RCC columns?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Standard RCC columns require a minimum clear cover of <strong>40 mm</strong> (or 25 mm if the column dimension is 200 mm or less and bar diameter is 12 mm or less) to safeguard rebar from fire hazards and environmental corrosion.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
